WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:10.530
Stories of blood-sucking monsters are universal
and have been around for centuries.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.299
There’s the Greek lamiae, the Chinese jiangshi,
the African asanbosam, and the Australian

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:16.580
yah-mah-ya-who to name a few.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:23.430
But let’s focus on the one who has outlived
them all, whose monstrous legend lives among

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.740
us even today—Count Dracula.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.780
The stories of blood-sucking monsters constantly
shifts to reflect the culture and issues of

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:41.780
its time.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.640
For instance, there are real-life diseases
with symptoms similar to the traits found

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.489
in some vampires: sensitivity to light, a
sudden decline in health, even the desire

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:52.000
to bite other people.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.229
So, before we understood concepts like viruses
and germs, creating a fictional explanation

00:00:57.229 --> 00:00:58.969
makes a lot of sense.

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:04.330
Also, if you look at the sharp teeth and long
fingernails of the typical western vampire,

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.799
and how they use these to attack their prey,
the vampire becomes a metaphor for a human’s

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.600
capacity for great violence.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.800
Vampires often appear humanoid and primarily
attack humans, so associating their violent

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.850
attacks with the violence we see in the real
world is easy—because both predator and

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.729
prey look like us.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:27.700
We can find this monster in folklore, legends,
and literature long before the word “vampire”

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.270
appears for the first time in English around
1730.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:36.359
However, it wasn’t until the Irish author
Abraham ‘Bram’ Stoker wrote his 1897 novel

00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:40.960
Dracula, that the characteristics of this
creature became widely recognizable in the

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.579
modern world.

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:47.079
Stoker actually started outlining the novel
in 1890, years before he even encountered

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:48.079
the name ‘Dracula.’

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:51.900
We know this because he wrote notes, a lot
of notes.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.460
Emily Gerard’s book of Transylvanian superstitions
The land beyond the forest and Reverend Sabine

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.680
Baring-Gould’s The Book of Were-Wolves were
two books which clearly inspired him.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.490
He clipped newspaper articles, recorded tombstone
inscriptions, and transcribed ship captain's

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.770
logbooks to make his narrative more realistic.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.830
He was also influenced by Victorian theatre,
including his friend and employer - the actor

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:14.830
Henry Irving.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:15.830
Stoker’s original list of characters shows
the famous vampire was first only known as

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.830
the “Count.”

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.720
It is most likely that he read the name “Dracula”
for the first time in William Wilkinson’s

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:25.760
book An Account of the Principalities of Wallachia
and Moldavia while on vacation with his family,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.050
after he had already started writing the story.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:33.680
In tradition with a lot of Gothic literature,
Dracula is a member of the aristocracy, which

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.740
also explains the dramatic castle setting,
and the Count’s great wealth of “old gold.”

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.850
In his memos, Stoker combined many existing
literary and folklore traits that we now see

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:49.710
as typical vampire characteristics: no reflection
in mirrors, never eats or drinks, has enormous

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.881
strength, and the ability to see in the dark.

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:57.150
It was already accepted that vampires could
turn others into the undead, have large canine

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.350
teeth and pointed nails, and be vulnerable
to garlic and wooden stakes.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:06.510
But Count Dracula was the first vampire to
have all of these traits.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:11.400
And influenced by werewolf legends, Stoker
gives Dracula the ability to shapeshift into

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.240
a bat, a wolf, or mist, a first for vampires!

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.540
Seven years of making vampire notes paid off,
and when the book was finally published, it

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.640
was a critical and popular success.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:27.110
The 1922 movie Nosferatu, which tells the
Dracula story with a few names changes, was

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:31.650
not authorized by Stoker and came dangerously
close to copyright infringement.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:36.440
Stoker’s widow even tried to have the film
removed from public circulation.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:41.940
The controversy surrounding the film increased
the popularity of both the book and the Count

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.940
himself.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.300
The prevelance of Dracula movies in the 40’s,
inspired a 16-year-old Richard Matheson, to

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:53.670
contemplate his own version of a vampire tale:
he wondered quote “if one vampire was scary,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.220
a world filled with vampires would be really
scary.”

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:02.780
Matheson published I am Legend, in 1954, telling
the story of the only apparent human survivor

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.360
in New York City after a vampire plague infects
the population.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.740
Matheson’s vampires becomes monsters not
from a bite or curse, but because of the Vampiris

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:13.740
virus.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:18.970
This is one of the first times the metaphor
of vampirism as a disease is explicitly stated.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:25.060
Urbanism, immigration, sexual transmitted
disease, politics, corporate greed, capitalism,

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:30.050
racism, sexism, the fetishization of youth—
these are only a few of the things vampires

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.050
have represented.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:36.410
In 1975, Stephen King’s Salem’s Lot modernizes
Stoker’s original story.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:41.250
King admits he was inspired by Dracula, as
well as the divisive political atmosphere

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:45.660
at that time in the United States that in
King’s own words gave him a quote “fear

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.190
of the future.”

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:53.460
Fun fact: thanks to the window-scratching
scene in the tv-movie version of Salem’s

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.050
Lot, the first monsters I remember being really
scared of were vampires.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:03.690
Which, given that I am now an expert in the
undead, is so perfectly ironic I could die.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:04.690
And then reanimate.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:09.720
It wouldn’t be a post-Dracula vampire episode
if I didn’t mention the two names that made

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.420
the modern vampire “sexy”…Anne Rice’s
Vampire Chronicles and Stephenie Meyer’s

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:15.420
Twilight series.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:21.460
In 1976, the publication of Interview with
a Vampire introduced the world to the beautiful,

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.640
soulful Louis, whose cold dead heart is still
capable of love and regret.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.900
Rice gave us the first reluctant vampires
in literature, those who were more concerned

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.120
with self-identity and morality than any previously
portrayed.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:45.830
Then in 2003, we were given the brooding,
abstinent, “vegetarian” Edward Cullen

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:46.910
in Twilight.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:51.380
Meyer makes turning someone into a vampire
the most romantic thing you can do because

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.580
it ensures you and your true love will be
together forever.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.240
Also, vampires now sparkle.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.220
Thanks for that.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.169
Other vampire stories reframe the undead monster
in exciting ways.

00:06:02.169 --> 00:06:07.250
Jewelle Gomez’s The Gilda Stories features
a black, feminist vampire heroine who uses

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:12.260
her undead life to explore her education and
her sexuality while helping to create progressive

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:13.960
change in society.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:19.440
Octavia Butler’s Fledgling features vampires
who actually engage in symbiotic relationships

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.940
with those they feed from.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.980
The narrative addresses themes of polyamory,
intimacy, race, and genetic experimentation

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.910
through the eyes of a black female protagonist.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:33.380
In contrast, Guillermo del Toro’s The Strain
trilogy gives us parasitic worms who inhabit

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:38.290
living human bodies, and whose failure to
value human life makes them monstrous even

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.870
without their need for human blood.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.400
Even though all modern vampire stories have
their roots in Stoker’s original Dracula,

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.340
each one is unique in its interpretation.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.910
As times change, so do vampires.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:54.640
The vampire reflects the culture and time
of its creation.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.040
I wonder what form they’ll take next?

